WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:17.940
My name is Aaron Cotton,
and I am the owner

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.268
and operator of Dryer Vent
Superheroes of New Orleans.

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:23.310
My name is Sophia Giordano-Scott.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.230
I am the co-owner and business manager

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.543
of Dryer Vent Superheroes of New Orleans.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.097
Aaron: Today, we're going to look at

00:00:30.097 --> 00:00:32.793
how to clean out a dusty dryer vent.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.420
The main thing that gets
basically conglomerated

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.200
in the vent pipes is a
lot of dead skin cells,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.447
pet hair, children's
glitter, regular hair,

00:00:43.447 --> 00:00:45.988
just dust, dryer detergent.

00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:48.779
Anything that can stay
and stick on our clothes,

00:00:48.779 --> 00:00:50.270
that can come off in the wash.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.180
Sophia: The worst is always
going to be any pet clinics.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.530
You're cleaning towels daily,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:56.820
and you're not just cleaning towels,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.890
but you're cleaning
towels full of pet hair,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.960
which is usually thicker and coarser

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.750
and sometimes more oily than human hair.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.610
Aaron: The metal brush
not only breaks it up,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.350
but it kind of catches as well.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.975
So it caught all that hair,

00:01:08.975 --> 00:01:10.933
and you can see as I pull it out,

00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:14.820
it's just almost a whole dog
that it seems like I pull out.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.060
Sophia: It was literally just
mounds of hardened pet hair

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.610
and lint inside all the way to the exit.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.520
And then that exit had
about 4 feet of bird's nest

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.350
just compacted at that end

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.150
because there was no
vent cover on the end.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.320
It was just an open tube.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.805
So it took us about two
hours to clean the vent

00:01:31.805 --> 00:01:33.720
in entirety, get everything out.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.960
We scoped it with our little
camera about six times

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.863
trying to make sure we got
every little piece out of there.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.350
Aaron: As the lint just
gets stuck in there

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.676
and it builds, over time,
the heat can't get out,

00:01:45.676 --> 00:01:47.687
so the heat just stays there,

00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:50.700
and it accumulates right
there, and it condenses,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.403
and that's actually how a fire will start.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.750
Sophia: Part of our
inspection when we first go in

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.630
is to make sure, is this vent
connected within the wall?

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.190
We only use these types of vents.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.364
They are metal but also flexible,

00:02:04.364 --> 00:02:06.656
but they're the only type
of connection hose vents

00:02:06.656 --> 00:02:08.430
that can actually contain a fire,

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.110
as opposed to the foil vents.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.580
If you look on the back of your dryer,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.280
most dryers will say
don't use a foil vent.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.350
And most people just
don't read that warning.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.580
Aaron: We get behind the dryer,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.130
and we take the metal tube
that's behind the dryer,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.680
and we take it off, and
we inspect to make sure

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.440
that the inside is either dirty or clean,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.893
because you either want to
clean it out or replace it.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.790
The second step would be to put our rods

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.550
with the brushes into
the wall with the camera.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.580
We can see inside the
wall of what's going on,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.590
and the customer can see as well

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.810
of either how dirty or how clean it is.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.030
You can definitely tell
almost how long it's been,

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.160
because if it's only
been, like, a year or two,

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.380
maybe a little or nothing at all.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.498
But when we're getting to the
three to five years or plus,

00:02:53.498 --> 00:02:56.403
you're getting literal mounds
of stuff that's coming out.

00:02:56.403 --> 00:02:58.980
You know, it's almost like
from a molehill to a mountain

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.110
of stuff that you're getting
out, and you're like, wow.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.993
Sophia: It's really gross
if it's 10-plus years.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.630
Aaron: So, after we inspect
it and see how dirty it is,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.880
then we take our brushes with our rods,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.080
so that goes inside the wall,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.783
and our drill basically
just cleans everything out.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.460
Sophia: We call this
the vent-polisher brush.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.710
It has these metal teeth at the end,

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.210
so when that goes through your vent,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.550
that's going to polish your
vents as well as clean it.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.720
So it's going to make it even harder

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.320
for lint to stick in the future.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.510
Aaron: When we take our
HEPA-filter vacuum cleaner,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.520
we make sure that no dust gets in the air

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.893
for either us or the
customer to breathe in.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.790
No cleaning solutions or chemicals.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.330
Just the brushes and the
HEPA-filter vacuum cleaner

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:46.443
really does the job.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.770
And then once all that's done and clean,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.990
we turn towards the inside of the dryers.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.900
We get all the lint that might
have been stuck in there.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.990
We go back and we take
our aluminum tube here,

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.780
and we put everything back
from the wall to the dryer.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.710
Sophia: We do use heat-safe
tape on all of our repairs

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.410
or connection hoses, and
we do also use clamps.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.460
The reason why we use both

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:09.960
is because you want your dryer vent

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.509
to be able to be unhooked
every year and cleaned.

00:04:12.509 --> 00:04:13.920
Aaron: And we push the dryer back,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.560
and then we check under the lint trap.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.870
We've definitely found
a lot of weird stuff

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.050
coming from the dryers,
especially under the lint trap.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.360
I've had one employee,
as we were cleaning,

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.060
he pulled out a whole silver chain.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.110
I've found money, like,
$5 and a couple $1 bills.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.300
Sophia: And then of course
we get the not-so-fun stuff,

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.460
bird eggs and wasp nests.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.740
Aaron: That's usually towards
the outside of the house,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.900
not in the dryer itself.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.040
Sophia: Also storm damage.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.260
Whenever we have a bad storm, hurricane,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.780
and something gets impacted in the vent

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.460
or the roof comes off or something,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.260
you're probably going to
get water in your vent,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.450
in which case you need a professional

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.290
to come and take that out,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:53.940
because it's going to
be really nasty water

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.598
mixed in with all that lint.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.587
Aaron: So we've had
multiple customers tell us,

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:01.410
"I've been in this
house for over 10 years,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.110
and I haven't even thought
about cleaning it out."

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.030
And they're like, "Why
are my clothes still wet?"

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.950
A lot of the times it's not your dryer,

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.680
it's, in fact, that the
line is just clogged up

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.840
with all the lint and hair and everything.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:13.950
Sophia: The most important thing to do

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.660
with keeping up your
dryer vent is definitely

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.430
to get that annual professional cleaning,

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.020
but in addition to that,

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.920
cleaning your lint trap with
every use is the biggest one.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.020
Aaron: I would say the most satisfying

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.040
is leaving with the customers
all happy and smiling

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.700
and knowing that they're safe,
that their house is safe,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.467
and that their product is going
to work as they want it to.

